{
  "abstract": "Ordinary covers of a ten-chromatic source can disperse its topological obstruction while retaining complicated coloring boundaries. We prove four nine-color extension results for the 25-vertex source G25, whose host K7 has ports of sizes 2,2,3 joined to three K5 arms and a common center K3. First, every cover with intact light columns is nine-colorable exactly when it has no trivial source component, with arbitrary center dispersion and center cycles. Second, when internal hosts remain K7s, prescribing three distinct center-source colors extends exactly when no light component is intact, with arbitrary arm components and host-to-arm matchings. Third, allowing one source of the three-vertex port to split yields an arbitrary prescribed-center extension theorem on split light components. This coordinates intact and split components that share centers and again leaves only trivial source components as obstructions. Fourth, two split sources admit arbitrary prescribed-center extension whenever nonadjacent row pairs can be selected to partition the heads. A cycle palette lemma removes an unnecessary injective assignment restriction in this last construction. The proofs combine exact local Hall criteria, source-incidence matchings, equality reservations, and constraint elimination that preserves binary palette conditions. Separate standard-library checkers verify 23 complete cover colorings and three abstract certificates; broader deterministic experiments test the constructions. The infinite statements rest on the proofs, and the controls are not claimed biplanar. Arbitrary ordinary covers and the unrestricted Earth–Moon problem remain outside the results.",
